00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:31.750
GANG CHEN: OK, hope you
all had a good weekend.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:35.160
And if you recall,
last time, we were

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.820
talking about the
Fermi-Dirac distribution,

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.220
Bose-Einstein distribution.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:48.090
And as I say, this is the
average number of particles

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:51.240
in one quantum state.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:56.310
So a certain energy, and
with a certain temperature,

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:59.340
and with certain
chemical potential.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:05.310
And we state that for
phonons and photons,

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:11.550
thermal equilibrium, the
camera potential mu equals 0.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:17.760
However, I caution you, if you
do in light-emitting devices,

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.140
or if you do solar
cells, the [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:28.290
potential of photons, the
emitted photons, is not 0.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:33.060
And that's because in this
case, the photon is interacting

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.800
with the electrons
holes, and you have,

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.260
let's say, electron holes
that have not the equilibrium,

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.600
and that's in this
case, you actually

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.640
have a mu not equaling 0.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.990
And that's why the light emitted
from a light-emitting diode

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:54.720
or laser can be much higher
than the black body radiation

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.100
intensity.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:03.420
And from there, once we
have this average number

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:08.970
of particles, we will discuss
the applications essentially

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:12.270
what we have discussed now
is the quantum mechanics

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:18.300
tells me, what's the energy
of any matter I give you?

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.750
A crystal, what's the
electron energy levels?

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.810
What's the phono energy
level, so a molecule or atom?

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.630
In principle, we can solve
this for the quantum mechanical

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:30.300
allowable ground state energy.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.880
There's no temperature there.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:40.650
And then, we add it to each
quantum state, the distribution

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:44.780
function, f, that's how many--

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.150
so this is the energy
of a quantum state,

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:51.500
this is how many particles we
have at that quantum state.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:56.040
And then, we discuss
the density of states,

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.760
that's per near
that energy level,

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.220
how many of those
quantum states?

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:03.740
So we're doing accounting.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:07.760
And by this time, what
I'm hoping is using--

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.430
so we show the black
body radiation,

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.620
in that case is a [INAUDIBLE].

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:14.810
And the Bose-Einstein
distribution density

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:16.440
of the state of photons.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.640
And you can easily say
the black-body radiation

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:22.430
are based on this expression.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:26.070
If you need a chair there,
other chairs are in the front.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:29.390
And then if you take--

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:32.390
so this gives the
energy density, right?

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:36.350
And if you take a temperature
derivative of that energy

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:39.830
density, you get
the specific heat.

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:44.120
Although for photons we seldom
talk about specific heat,

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.930
and that's why for photons
the lattice we can measure,

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:50.720
so we use the specific heat.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.350
And the low temperature
you get T-cubic power.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:58.250
And that's very similar to
what is black-body radiation

00:03:58.250 --> 00:03:59.930
normal T-force power.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:02.300
That's the energy and the
temperature derivative

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.450
to give you T-cubic power.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:09.120
So by the end of
the last lecture,

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.790
I said I'm going to tell you I
could just stop here and move

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:14.880
to the next chapter.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.459
But I'm going to use
half of today's lecture

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:23.690
to tell you the statistical
foundation because there

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:26.078
is some very useful language.

00:04:26.078 --> 00:04:27.620
I'm not going to
test you, as I said.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.670
But I thought it was
really beautiful.

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.680
So it's good that
you have some-- you

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:35.370
know the terminologies here.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:41.550
So in statistics what
do we want if I have,

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:47.030
for example, a system where
the equilibrium surrounding,

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:49.760
I want to measure
its energy, or I want

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:51.420
to measure its temperature.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:54.230
So what I have is
a time average.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:57.200
I do a certain
amount of measurement

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:00.590
over a certain period of
time because instantaneously

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:04.180
those quantities
are fluctuating.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:08.920
So one way to find a
quality that we report

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:11.690
is the time average.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:17.950
But in statistical
mechanics the framework

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:22.330
is developed most along
the ensemble average.

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:23.750
There is a time average.

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:27.280
For example, if you do
molecular dynamics simulation,

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.070
you actually solve
the equation of motion

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:32.290
and you get the
trajectory of the atoms,

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.870
and you see how to
do the averaging.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:39.790
And the ensemble
average is based on not

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:41.920
considering time history--

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.640
so if I have a system, there
are many quantum mechanics

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:48.970
that say, OK, I can go
to solve in principle--

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:50.890
Schrodinger
equation-- I find all

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:54.610
quantum states of the system.

00:05:54.610 --> 00:06:02.920
So I have quantum state one
and all those quantum states.

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:09.370
And this omega is
typically much, much larger

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:12.900
than the number of
particles you have.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:19.470
In fact, we'll see soon even
one atom, the quantum state--

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:23.310
say the allowable quantum state
in this room for one molecule--

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:25.980
will be much, much larger
than the total number

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:28.250
of molecules in this room.

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:33.230
So this is a huge number, omega.

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:37.370
And the ensemble
average is actually

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:42.690
looking at each of these quantum
states, the time evolution.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:46.150
But right now let's
not think about time.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:48.600
Each quantum state,
what's the probability?

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:52.030
So if you do a
statistic averaging,

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:59.650
what's the probability for
observing quantum state I?

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:00.150
Right?

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:07.070
And in that quantum state,
what's this quantity Xi?

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:11.360
For example, what's the
energy of that quantum state?

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.370
What's the momentum
of that quantum state?

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:15.520
Microscopic, right?

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:18.890
Where we're targeting.

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:20.990
And now the probability--

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:26.760
now for that, say one side of
the system in my ensemble--

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:31.750
this is a many state
form of ensemble, right?

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:35.830
So if I know the probability
of observing that quantum

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:41.080
state in the ensemble,
then now the value

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:44.650
that I'm interested in that
specific ensemble, then

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:46.870
my average--

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:54.190
the ensemble average--
is then Xi Pi and sum

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:59.260
over all the quantum states.

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:04.000
So this is the concept
of ensemble average.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:08.200
And that's in statistics
we do most of the time.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:11.740
We don't actually try to
solve the time history,

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:13.210
because time
history, like I said,

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:18.070
is only a tool that was
developed so a computer could

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:19.035
become very powerful.

00:08:19.035 --> 00:08:24.310
You saw, for example, in
molecular dynamics, right?

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:30.180
So there is a very
fundamental assumption

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:33.960
when these two different
ways of averaging

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.390
are equal to each other.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:42.590
A system, when the time average
equals the ensemble average,

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:43.820
that's called ergodic.

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:58.800
So ergodic system is
when the time average

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:02.290
equals the ensemble average.

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:04.435
And most systems are ergodic.

00:09:07.668 --> 00:09:12.540
It turns out that studying
long ergodic systems

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:19.040
is actually an active branch
for strategic physics.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:23.750
So ergodic system is
one that after some time

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:25.700
it tends to equilibrium.

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:27.155
The samples of all the states.

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:35.520
But there are
interesting examples

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:37.020
of long ergodic systems.

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:39.820
I can't help but
give you one example.

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:44.220
And this example is
a famous problem.

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:47.280
The FPU problem.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:49.192
It's a Fermi-Pasta-Ulam.

00:09:57.310 --> 00:10:03.370
So it turns out in the 1950s,
when the computer was just

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:10.810
becoming available,
Fermi-Pasta-Ulam, they

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:11.800
published a report.

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:14.780
It was another UN paper.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:18.620
And what they reported
is that they simulated

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:30.300
a one dimensional atomic
chain and the sprint

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:32.230
is not a harmonic.

00:10:32.230 --> 00:10:32.730
Right?

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:34.230
Before when we
started [INAUDIBLE],,

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:35.970
we started harmonic.

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:36.480
Kx.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:38.910
F equals Kx.

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:42.120
And they added a
higher order term.

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:44.850
A long linear term.

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:47.670
And what that long linear term--

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:50.250
this is what they, later
on, would discuss more--

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:52.770
is the cause for scattering.

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:55.770
If I have a perfect
crystal, or that spring,

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:59.660
just to say we have
the full picture there,

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:01.430
you have infinite
thermal conductivity

00:11:01.430 --> 00:11:04.290
because the waves are not
interacting with each other.

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:08.700
So if I have a crystal, I
create a disturbance here.

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:09.830
They do not interact.

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:11.990
They propagate infinitely.

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:16.280
So a perfect crystal with
a perfect harmonic spring

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:19.490
has infinite thermal
conductivity.

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:23.060
And most material has a
finite thermal conductivity

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:26.710
because those springs
are long, have

00:11:26.710 --> 00:11:29.150
a higher order of
terms that start

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:31.190
to interact in different modes.

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:32.180
That's a scattering.

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:34.740
That's what we're going
to talk about more.

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:39.260
So the reason they started
long harmonic spring

00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:45.940
is to look at the scattering
in this atomic chain.

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:49.550
But what they found
is very interesting.

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:51.610
And they found that if
they put a one mode--

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:54.500
put the energy into one mode--

00:11:54.500 --> 00:12:00.050
after some time the energy of
this mode gave to other modes.

00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:02.810
But after a long
time of simulating--

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:05.900
so if it's a normal
system, we're

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:07.500
going to approach equilibrium.

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:09.740
So every mode gathers
some energy, right?

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:15.110
You put the energy in
one mode, and eventually

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:18.950
equal partition and every
mode gets some energy.

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:21.900
But what they found is
that after some time,

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:25.250
all the energy came
back to this mode.

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:26.420
OK?

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:32.220
So what that implies is that
this system is not ergodic.

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:35.150
It doesn't approach
the equilibrium.

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:39.500
And so from that
what they imply--

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:42.530
I'm very interested in
this because they also

00:12:42.530 --> 00:12:46.960
imply this atomic chain has an
infinite thermal conductivity.

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:49.750
Energy never dissipates.

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:51.520
And that was actually
my motivation

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:55.640
when I got a call from
a friend at Intel.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:13:00.220
He said, we're looking at the
solution for heat spreading.

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:01.960
Thermal management.

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:03.328
And I look, and I say, OK.

00:13:03.328 --> 00:13:04.120
Thermal management.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:07.270
He said the scanning
noise is going to--

00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:09.850
Moore's law is going
to come to an end.

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:13.570
We're going to make, rather
than just shrink the gap,

00:13:13.570 --> 00:13:16.440
but we're going to
make more products.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:18.240
So I look at my
cell phone, right?

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:23.010
Or if you look at your
iPad, it's all metal here.

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:25.890
And some of the old
cell phones are plastic.

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:27.380
AUDIENCE: That's glass.

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:28.050
GANG CHEN: Huh?

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:29.008
AUDIENCE: That's glass.

00:13:29.008 --> 00:13:30.360
GANG CHEN: Not glass.

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:31.320
They're metals.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:33.840
This is pretty heavy.

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:38.430
Well it's actually-- so the iPad
has aluminum in the backing.

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:40.200
AUDIENCE: Yeah, but
that backing is glass.

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:41.492
GANG CHEN: These are all glass?

00:13:41.492 --> 00:13:42.850
AUDIENCE: The backing is glass.

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:45.225
GANG CHEN: Yeah, but there
should be a metal here, right?

00:13:45.225 --> 00:13:48.330
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:54.110
But basically, metal is
used to spread out heat.

00:13:54.110 --> 00:13:56.560
You say why your
iPad the backside,

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:59.650
there's aluminum to
spread out the heat.

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:03.910
So I say, can I turn polymer
into a good heat conductor?

00:14:03.910 --> 00:14:07.030
So I said, oh, I
remember FPU problem.

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:10.630
Maybe a polymer chain is
an infinite heat conductor.

00:14:10.630 --> 00:14:14.110
So that's how we actually
started some polymer work,

00:14:14.110 --> 00:14:16.510
and we were able
to turn polymer--

00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:19.150
at least made it as
good as most metals.

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:22.000
We achieved a 300
times improvement

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:24.940
in the molecular
chain direction.

00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:26.140
That's just a fiber.

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:27.800
A single molecule
we don't know yet.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:31.000
The theory-- the calculations
may even be much higher.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:32.770
Maybe even divergent.

00:14:32.770 --> 00:14:35.840
We have not been
able to measure it.

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:39.320
So think about the fundamental
aspects that sometimes give you

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:44.040
motivation in which directions
you want to look at.

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:48.830
But that's just-- there
are not many systems that

00:14:48.830 --> 00:14:49.930
are long ergodic.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:55.900
Most systems are ergodic, so we
can have the ensemble average

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:57.505
equals time average.

00:15:08.020 --> 00:15:14.060
With this I'm going to say
there are different ensembles.

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:15.530
OK?

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:20.600
And I'm going to discuss
three major ensembles.

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:23.210
You can construct
your own ensemble.

00:15:23.210 --> 00:15:28.460
So it depends on the macroscopic
constraint to the total system.

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:30.350
There are three types.

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:36.230
One is, I give you a system,
and that's a closed system.

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:41.540
And I fix the total number
of particles, the volume,

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:42.365
and the energy.

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:49.620
So this ensemble has a
fixed amount of energy,

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:54.330
a fixed amount of numbers
of particles, and volume.

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:57.180
And that's an isolated system.

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:01.920
Energy is a constant,
and it doesn't

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:03.630
interact with the surrounding.

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:05.010
Volume is constant.

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:07.470
Number of particles is constant.

00:16:07.470 --> 00:16:09.580
That's an isolated system.

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:13.120
So it turns out that
the probability--

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:15.000
So what I'm looking
at is, what's

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:18.330
the probability of
observing a quantum

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:27.270
state of this macroscopic system
which has many quantum states?

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:30.520
So this is one
type of constraint.

00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:32.790
And the next type
of constraint, is

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:37.520
I don't do an isolated system.

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:40.100
Rather than the
energy as a constant,

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:43.370
I have the temperature
as a constant.

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:50.040
So I have N, V, T.
And in this case,

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:53.120
temperature is constant so
the energy can fluctuate,

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:59.900
and it can interact with
the surrounding environment.

00:16:59.900 --> 00:17:02.600
Let's say the environment is
at a constant temperature.

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:07.460
And the third is a
system that I don't need

00:17:07.460 --> 00:17:08.940
to fix the number of particles.

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:12.589
It can have mass exchange,
particle exchange,

00:17:12.589 --> 00:17:15.319
with it's surroundings.

00:17:15.319 --> 00:17:19.440
So if the particles
are in exchange--

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:21.640
either equilibrium
when my system is--

00:17:21.640 --> 00:17:24.579
the thermal equilibrium,
temperature is equal.

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:29.430
When a system has mass
equilibrium, the particle

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:33.890
equilibrium, the chemical
potential is equal.

00:17:33.890 --> 00:17:36.560
So I deal with the
system the [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:39.980
potential of volume,
temperature is fixed.

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:41.848
The number of particles
are not fixed.

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:49.990
So now for each of these,
I can take a system

00:17:49.990 --> 00:17:52.690
and I make an ensemble.

00:17:52.690 --> 00:17:58.510
And in this case I make an
ensemble of quantum state I.

00:17:58.510 --> 00:18:05.410
I for quantum state, that has
a fixed number, N, V, U. What's

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:08.890
the probability of observing
this quantum state?

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:13.270
That's the P. That's
what I want to find out.

00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:22.650
And similarly, for this one,
again I is quantum state.

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:24.030
What's the probability?

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:31.050
But in this case, I have N,
I have V, I have T. Right?

00:18:31.050 --> 00:18:34.190
I do not have fixed energy.

00:18:34.190 --> 00:18:38.600
And this system may have
a specific energy Ei,

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:42.590
rather than a given energy.

00:18:42.590 --> 00:18:48.440
What's the probability of
observing this quantum state--

00:18:48.440 --> 00:18:53.370
specific quantum state--
in the whole ensemble?

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:56.970
I have many, many
of such states.

00:18:56.970 --> 00:18:59.850
And the same is for this.

00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:08.010
In this case, the I quantum
state can have Ei, Ni.

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:11.890
The particle is also not fixed.

00:19:11.890 --> 00:19:18.110
And I have mu, V, T. So
those are the questions

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:19.610
we want to answer.

00:19:19.610 --> 00:19:22.490
The probability of Pi.

00:19:26.630 --> 00:19:29.060
And here it's Ei.

00:19:29.060 --> 00:19:37.040
And the rest N, V, T. And
here is P, Ei, and Ni.

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:41.600
And the rest of the
fixed quantity, mu, N, T.

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:44.840
So we want to find out
what's the probability.

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:49.730
And we have specific names for
the three different constraints

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:51.410
in the ensemble form.

00:19:51.410 --> 00:19:53.250
Here is a microcanonical.

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:04.920
And here is canonical.

00:20:07.990 --> 00:20:09.565
And here is grand canonical.

00:20:09.565 --> 00:20:12.310
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:12.310 --> 00:20:14.350
GANG CHEN: What's the
name you want again?

00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:18.940
AUDIENCE: I'm just guessing
macro, but grand is better.

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:20.080
GANG CHEN: OK.

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:21.070
Grand canonical.

00:20:21.070 --> 00:20:25.170
So it's three different
canonical ensembles.

00:20:25.170 --> 00:20:27.110
OK.

00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:30.980
And the starting point
is the microcanonical.

00:20:30.980 --> 00:20:35.240
And once you settle
on that microcanonical

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:39.080
you can derive all the
distribution probability

00:20:39.080 --> 00:20:42.400
for all other canonical systems.

00:20:42.400 --> 00:20:55.820
So the microcanonical ensemble--

00:20:59.220 --> 00:21:08.610
So if I have a total of omega
systems in this ensemble,

00:21:08.610 --> 00:21:09.450
microcanonical.

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:11.490
Right?

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:15.210
And the probability
for microcanonical--

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:19.500
there's no proof,
it's a principle--

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:24.000
is that for such a system
everybody has equal right.

00:21:26.600 --> 00:21:29.190
So equal probability.

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:33.500
So in this case it's
equal probability

00:21:33.500 --> 00:21:37.250
because I have a total
of omega quantum state.

00:21:37.250 --> 00:21:41.130
Each quantum state
is 1 over omega.

00:21:41.130 --> 00:21:46.560
So this is an equal
probability assumption.

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:52.190
And based on this
it was Boltzmann

00:21:52.190 --> 00:21:54.920
who said for such a system--

00:21:54.920 --> 00:22:08.500
The Boltzmann
principal collected

00:22:08.500 --> 00:22:14.750
the number of
ensembles in the system

00:22:14.750 --> 00:22:20.100
is a direct measure of the
entropy of this system.

00:22:20.100 --> 00:22:23.630
So the Boltzmann
principal says the entropy

00:22:23.630 --> 00:22:28.320
of such a system,
which is a function

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:33.900
of the macroscopic
constraint N, V, U--

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:45.490
so entropy is a function of N,
V, U, which is Kb log omega.

00:22:45.490 --> 00:22:47.214
That's the Boltzmann principle.

00:22:49.820 --> 00:22:53.900
Boltzmann was very depressed.

00:22:53.900 --> 00:23:00.350
He was a great scientist and
didn't get the recognition.

00:23:00.350 --> 00:23:04.120
He actually jumped in to a lake.

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:06.820
And so if you have a
chance to go to Vienna,

00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:10.470
his tombstone is the
S equals Kb log omega.

00:23:15.660 --> 00:23:18.855
So what they say
here, is the entropy--

00:23:21.485 --> 00:23:25.400
with this one you have to also
take a lot of probability.

00:23:25.400 --> 00:23:29.000
And by taking a
logarithm, you actually

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:32.210
make the entropy
additive quantity.

00:23:32.210 --> 00:23:36.110
If you have two subsystems,
the total entropy of the system

00:23:36.110 --> 00:23:37.160
is the entropy--

00:23:37.160 --> 00:23:41.070
the sum of the entropy
of each system.

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:44.070
Because if you have two
systems, each one has the omega,

00:23:44.070 --> 00:23:47.940
the total combination of the
state is omega 1 times omega 2.

00:23:47.940 --> 00:23:53.120
So that makes the log
give you the summation.

00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:58.820
So the fact that I
have functioning S,

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:04.610
is a functional of N,V, U, this
is called the thermodynamic

00:24:04.610 --> 00:24:05.480
function.

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:06.860
Thermodynamic potential.

00:24:19.520 --> 00:24:25.050
So it turns out for a
microcanonical ensemble,

00:24:25.050 --> 00:24:28.030
the thermodynamic
potential is the entropy.

00:24:28.030 --> 00:24:30.880
If you know entropy as
a function of N, V, U,

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:34.700
you know everything
about this system.

00:24:34.700 --> 00:24:39.430
But if you know this as a
function of other variables,

00:24:39.430 --> 00:24:44.340
like mu, or temperature, you do
not have that right potential.

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:45.950
You do not have all
the information.

00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:50.040
So why is that?

00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:54.650
So if I say I know
the S of the system,

00:24:54.650 --> 00:24:59.300
I can find all other quantities
because if I look at this S

00:24:59.300 --> 00:25:01.470
is a function of N, V, U--

00:25:01.470 --> 00:25:07.430
So I have ds, du.

00:25:07.430 --> 00:25:11.750
And I can keep my N,
my weight, constant.

00:25:11.750 --> 00:25:18.140
du, if I write it
into partial, ds, dv,

00:25:18.140 --> 00:25:27.910
and I write U and N
constant, dv plus ds dn,

00:25:27.910 --> 00:25:32.730
and I have U,
weight constant, dN.

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:36.640
This is just
partial derivatives.

00:25:36.640 --> 00:25:38.710
And if you go to
think about what

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:40.750
you write in typical
thermodynamics,

00:25:40.750 --> 00:25:44.200
you write U equals
Tds minus a Pdv.

00:25:46.850 --> 00:25:52.520
So with that I can say,
really, what I have, ds and du,

00:25:52.520 --> 00:25:57.260
is 1 over T du.

00:25:57.260 --> 00:26:01.260
So if I know this function,
I know the temperature.

00:26:01.260 --> 00:26:09.010
And a ds dv is
really P over T dv.

00:26:09.010 --> 00:26:11.960
So now the pressure and--

00:26:11.960 --> 00:26:18.350
say ds, dn is the chemical
potential over T dn.

00:26:18.350 --> 00:26:21.710
And using this
you could write it

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:23.570
into your more familiar form.

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:32.630
This is a du equals a Tds,
minus a pdv, and plus mu dn.

00:26:32.630 --> 00:26:36.300
Right, this is the form that
you're more familiar with.

00:26:36.300 --> 00:26:37.610
So what I miss--

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:42.320
if I know this function, I
can find mu, I can find P,

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:44.325
I can find T of this system.

00:26:44.325 --> 00:26:45.950
So I know everything
about this system.

00:26:50.910 --> 00:27:01.330
But typically we do not do
microcanonical ensembles.

00:27:01.330 --> 00:27:04.540
Microcanonical ensembles
are harder to work with.

00:27:04.540 --> 00:27:07.690
And many times we like
to work with a system

00:27:07.690 --> 00:27:11.720
at a constant temperature.

00:27:11.720 --> 00:27:16.340
Or if you recall when
we deal with electrons,

00:27:16.340 --> 00:27:19.640
we say, OK, I take one
system, and there could

00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:21.290
be electrons in, electrons out.

00:27:21.290 --> 00:27:25.730
So that's how it's right, the
Fermi-Dirac, Bose-Einstein

00:27:25.730 --> 00:27:27.440
distributions.

00:27:27.440 --> 00:27:32.010
So that's actually the result
of grand canonical ensemble.

00:27:32.010 --> 00:27:35.840
So let's move on
to say, if I have

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:39.950
here a microcanonical
ensemble, what

00:27:39.950 --> 00:27:42.050
would be the canonical
ensemble probability?

00:27:51.050 --> 00:27:54.550
Again I want to find what's
the probability of a quantum

00:27:54.550 --> 00:27:58.420
system, a quantum state,
that has energy Ei.

00:28:01.690 --> 00:28:04.300
But unlike
microcanonical ensemble

00:28:04.300 --> 00:28:09.310
in which every system
has equal rights,

00:28:09.310 --> 00:28:10.960
this one is no
longer equal right.

00:28:13.820 --> 00:28:20.290
And if we want to use what we
just learned on microcanonical

00:28:20.290 --> 00:28:23.560
to derive an expression
for the probability

00:28:23.560 --> 00:28:27.210
in this canonical
ensemble, what you do

00:28:27.210 --> 00:28:31.480
is you enclose the environment.

00:28:31.480 --> 00:28:34.670
Because this system
is not isolated.

00:28:34.670 --> 00:28:36.760
But if I enclose
the environment--

00:28:39.360 --> 00:28:40.770
here is the environment.

00:28:44.850 --> 00:28:49.515
And all together this
is a closed system.

00:28:53.690 --> 00:28:58.205
And I have one quantum
state of the system itself.

00:28:58.205 --> 00:29:00.500
I have the energy, Ei.

00:29:00.500 --> 00:29:07.050
The environment has
many, many other states.

00:29:07.050 --> 00:29:12.630
So the environment that will
have omega i quantum state.

00:29:12.630 --> 00:29:15.670
So I have to quantize
my environment

00:29:15.670 --> 00:29:18.920
because each
combination gives me

00:29:18.920 --> 00:29:21.935
a system in the
microcanonical ensemble.

00:29:26.150 --> 00:29:33.740
So if this omega t is the total
quantum state of a combined--

00:29:33.740 --> 00:29:36.860
total of a combined system
plus environment together--

00:29:39.620 --> 00:29:44.870
System plus environment
together, that gives me

00:29:44.870 --> 00:29:47.160
the total number of states.

00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:55.580
And then omega i is when
the system is at one quantum

00:29:55.580 --> 00:30:00.470
state, the i's quantum state
with the energy Ei, what's

00:30:00.470 --> 00:30:04.360
a number of states
of the environment?

00:30:04.360 --> 00:30:08.718
So if this is omega t,
and the combined is Ut--

00:30:08.718 --> 00:30:09.760
that's the total energy--

00:30:12.930 --> 00:30:22.000
and the entropy of the combined
system and environment is St.

00:30:22.000 --> 00:30:28.780
And in this case, the
energy, because I have--

00:30:28.780 --> 00:30:31.300
the combined the
system plus environment

00:30:31.300 --> 00:30:34.660
is my isolated system.

00:30:34.660 --> 00:30:40.700
So when the system itself
at the Ei, the energy

00:30:40.700 --> 00:30:44.610
of the environment Ut minus Ei.

00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:47.250
They add together and gave
me constant Ut, right?

00:30:47.250 --> 00:30:50.040
So Ut minus Ei.

00:30:53.090 --> 00:30:57.620
That's the energy
of the environment.

00:30:57.620 --> 00:30:59.000
So my probability--

00:31:03.560 --> 00:31:08.870
This is the quantum
states of environment.

00:31:11.870 --> 00:31:17.790
So my probability of
counting the system, my Ei,

00:31:17.790 --> 00:31:20.250
a certain temperature,
now of course,

00:31:20.250 --> 00:31:22.460
I can write as a volume.

00:31:22.460 --> 00:31:24.530
Say, Lambert density.

00:31:24.530 --> 00:31:28.580
My microscopic constraint is 1.

00:31:28.580 --> 00:31:31.490
That's the one quantum
state of the system.

00:31:31.490 --> 00:31:37.570
How many states this
will total comp--

00:31:37.570 --> 00:31:41.650
how much did the environment
that also has the--

00:31:41.650 --> 00:31:45.940
say, when this system
is at the energy, Ei.

00:31:45.940 --> 00:31:49.780
And divide that by omega t.

00:31:49.780 --> 00:31:50.470
That's total.

00:31:55.500 --> 00:31:58.290
So now I need to do
a little bit of math

00:31:58.290 --> 00:32:02.280
using the fact that it's
a microcanoncial ensemble

00:32:02.280 --> 00:32:05.890
to derive this probability.

00:32:05.890 --> 00:32:10.140
So let me say here is omega i.

00:32:10.140 --> 00:32:17.940
I have this energy, but my real
average energy of the system

00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:21.690
is U. Ei is just as
specific quantum state.

00:32:21.690 --> 00:32:24.570
So what's the energy
of that state, right?

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:26.040
So the average energy--

00:32:26.040 --> 00:32:29.790
because in this case, the
average energy can fluctuate.

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:31.290
Because of the
constant temperature,

00:32:31.290 --> 00:32:33.600
energy can fluctuate.

00:32:33.600 --> 00:32:40.020
So I can write this Ut minus Ei,
in terms of Ut minus U, which

00:32:40.020 --> 00:32:42.240
is the average
energy of the system,

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:47.740
plus U minus Ei,
which is a deviation.

00:32:47.740 --> 00:32:52.080
U minus Ei is a deviation
from the equilibrium,

00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:52.905
the average energy.

00:32:57.730 --> 00:33:01.390
So that's just a function
I'm rewriting in variables.

00:33:01.390 --> 00:33:04.720
And divided that by omega t.

00:33:14.700 --> 00:33:17.160
I could write--
now I can go back

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:21.260
to use the Boltzmann principle.

00:33:21.260 --> 00:33:24.335
Omega and the S relation.

00:33:26.980 --> 00:33:33.130
So I have omega for the
environment at this energy.

00:33:33.130 --> 00:33:35.930
And omega for the total.

00:33:35.930 --> 00:33:44.880
So I can write it in terms of
S. So I have, if you look at it,

00:33:44.880 --> 00:33:45.910
it's an inversion.

00:33:45.910 --> 00:33:51.560
So it's exponential s when
the system, the energy

00:33:51.560 --> 00:33:57.860
is Ut minus U, plus U minus Ei.

00:33:57.860 --> 00:34:01.910
This is just a variable
inside divide by kb.

00:34:01.910 --> 00:34:04.670
And I have a log already.

00:34:04.670 --> 00:34:06.690
I have an exponential already.

00:34:06.690 --> 00:34:09.179
You will see the
Boltzmann factor.

00:34:09.179 --> 00:34:17.270
And here I have e s of the
total of the system, Ut and kb.

00:34:22.120 --> 00:34:31.170
So I'm using the result to
determine S and log of omega.

00:34:31.170 --> 00:34:34.050
And here I'm going to expand--

00:34:34.050 --> 00:34:36.449
this S I'm going to expand.

00:34:36.449 --> 00:34:41.010
The reason I write it into this
is that I'm going to expand--

00:34:41.010 --> 00:34:44.770
this is the equilibrium,
the average of the system.

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:47.159
This is the average
of the environment.

00:34:47.159 --> 00:34:54.110
Ut minus U. And this is a
deviation from that average.

00:34:54.110 --> 00:35:00.066
So I can write this in
terms of E equals S,

00:35:00.066 --> 00:35:12.410
equals S Ut, team minus U,
plus ds, du, and U minus Ei.

00:35:15.550 --> 00:35:16.940
That's a Taylor expansion.

00:35:16.940 --> 00:35:17.440
Right?

00:35:20.810 --> 00:35:34.760
ds, du, given the microcanonical
ensemble, ds, du is 1 over T.

00:35:34.760 --> 00:35:40.220
So now if you go one more
step, this is 1 over T.

00:35:40.220 --> 00:35:44.000
Here we have 1
over T, U minus Ei.

00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:54.770
So what I have then
is P, Ei, real the

00:35:54.770 --> 00:35:57.750
T. I don't need to carry
anyway, which is fixed.

00:35:57.750 --> 00:35:58.970
It doesn't change.

00:35:58.970 --> 00:36:17.760
So it's exponential S, Ut minus
U. And plus U minus Ei over T.

00:36:17.760 --> 00:36:24.960
And I should have kb, and
here I should also have kb,

00:36:24.960 --> 00:36:28.118
divide by exponential S, Ut.

00:36:39.550 --> 00:36:43.810
So the entropy-- the
average entropy here

00:36:43.810 --> 00:36:48.850
of the total system
minus the system.

00:36:48.850 --> 00:36:53.700
So this is really the
entropy of the environment.

00:36:53.700 --> 00:36:57.750
The entropy of the environment,
this is the total entropy.

00:36:57.750 --> 00:37:04.860
So S, Ut is the S,
Ut minus U. That's

00:37:04.860 --> 00:37:06.570
the entropy of the environment.

00:37:06.570 --> 00:37:09.570
S, U, that's the
entropy of the system.

00:37:09.570 --> 00:37:12.120
The additive
property of entropy.

00:37:15.270 --> 00:37:20.310
So with that, this S,
U cancels this one.

00:37:20.310 --> 00:37:33.300
And what I have is now
exponential U minus ST over kb.

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:34.590
That's one.

00:37:34.590 --> 00:37:37.470
Oh, kbT.

00:37:37.470 --> 00:37:44.310
And the other is the
exponential minus Ei over kbT.

00:37:44.310 --> 00:37:48.540
This I combine by using
the relation here.

00:37:52.430 --> 00:37:59.050
So this is the probability
of a canonical system,

00:37:59.050 --> 00:38:01.310
a canonical ensemble.

00:38:01.310 --> 00:38:06.400
When one quantum
state has energy, Ei,

00:38:06.400 --> 00:38:09.460
and the system is a temperature
T, what's the probability?

00:38:12.290 --> 00:38:18.190
And before, U is the
system's average energy,

00:38:18.190 --> 00:38:23.540
S is the system's average
entropy, and temperature.

00:38:23.540 --> 00:38:29.520
So this one is an independent
of each quantum state.

00:38:29.520 --> 00:38:33.450
Here is the quantity
for each quantum state.

00:38:33.450 --> 00:38:39.720
And I can write this as
exponential minus Ei,

00:38:39.720 --> 00:38:45.630
kbT over Z. And that's Z--

00:38:45.630 --> 00:38:49.070
1 over Z is really
this factor here.

00:38:52.020 --> 00:38:53.510
What is U minus S, T?

00:38:53.510 --> 00:38:55.470
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:38:55.470 --> 00:38:59.840
GANG CHEN: Yeah, that's what
they come for, free energy.

00:38:59.840 --> 00:39:08.290
F. F equals U minus S, T. So
my thermodynamic potential

00:39:08.290 --> 00:39:12.030
for a canonical
system is actually

00:39:12.030 --> 00:39:21.050
F. F is U minus S, T. If I
know F as a function of T,

00:39:21.050 --> 00:39:21.560
then wait.

00:39:21.560 --> 00:39:23.600
I need to know that function.

00:39:23.600 --> 00:39:27.290
F is a function of
N, V, T, then I know

00:39:27.290 --> 00:39:29.965
everything about this system.

00:39:33.180 --> 00:39:36.285
So let's do a few more steps.

00:39:48.260 --> 00:39:49.670
Here is my probability.

00:39:49.670 --> 00:39:53.510
Of course, I can find
out what's the relation

00:39:53.510 --> 00:39:56.000
between Z and the Ei?

00:39:56.000 --> 00:40:02.660
Because my probability
adds up P, Eim and t,

00:40:02.660 --> 00:40:05.210
that has to equal the sum
of all quantum states.

00:40:05.210 --> 00:40:06.670
It must be what?

00:40:06.670 --> 00:40:09.300
Normalization.

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:15.840
And from here, I can
say my Z is really

00:40:15.840 --> 00:40:20.160
sum over e minus Ei, kbT.

00:40:24.680 --> 00:40:32.460
And because F equals U minus T,
S, now I have Z. I have this.

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:33.920
And I can find--

00:40:33.920 --> 00:40:39.215
I can also write the
[INAUDIBLE] free energy here.

00:40:44.620 --> 00:40:47.930
So based on the
relationship I have here,

00:40:47.930 --> 00:40:51.310
the thermodynamic potential
for a canonical system,

00:40:51.310 --> 00:41:01.450
which is N, V, T, and if I this
function, which is log, kbT,

00:41:01.450 --> 00:41:03.190
log Z--

00:41:03.190 --> 00:41:04.430
this is the one I write.

00:41:04.430 --> 00:41:06.130
Rewrite this one.

00:41:06.130 --> 00:41:12.950
And I have F equals
kbT log Z. And if I

00:41:12.950 --> 00:41:19.788
know this free-form free energy,
as a functional of N, V, T,

00:41:19.788 --> 00:41:21.580
I should know everything
about this system.

00:41:24.850 --> 00:41:35.930
So you can say now the beauty
is this ensemble approach

00:41:35.930 --> 00:41:40.950
is, once we start the
microcanonical ensemble

00:41:40.950 --> 00:41:43.910
as a basic assumption
of equal probability,

00:41:43.910 --> 00:41:47.360
you can derive probability
for all other ensembles.

00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:54.480
And if you happen to
do molecular dynamics

00:41:54.480 --> 00:41:59.850
simulation, and you
say that at the end

00:41:59.850 --> 00:42:02.460
there is actually something--

00:42:02.460 --> 00:42:04.840
most people don't discuss this--

00:42:04.840 --> 00:42:07.630
is that when you
simulate the system,

00:42:07.630 --> 00:42:13.000
you typically simulate the
microcanonical ensemble.

00:42:13.000 --> 00:42:16.090
You simulate a system
with a fixed number

00:42:16.090 --> 00:42:19.250
of particles and total energy.

00:42:19.250 --> 00:42:21.700
And in fact you
spend a lot of time

00:42:21.700 --> 00:42:23.690
trying to maintain
your energy constant.

00:42:23.690 --> 00:42:25.960
Otherwise your
simulation blows up.

00:42:25.960 --> 00:42:27.670
It's not stable.

00:42:27.670 --> 00:42:32.440
But when you do analysis,
you actually go--

00:42:32.440 --> 00:42:34.660
this is, as I mentioned
before, you have

00:42:34.660 --> 00:42:37.540
to go to chapter 10 of my book.

00:42:37.540 --> 00:42:42.390
And you actually use the
canonical example result.

00:42:42.390 --> 00:42:44.400
And Boltzmann statistics.

00:42:44.400 --> 00:42:46.980
This is the Boltzmann
factor here.

00:42:46.980 --> 00:42:49.320
We mentioned it before.

00:42:49.320 --> 00:42:57.330
So there is an inherent
inconsistency in this approach.

00:42:57.330 --> 00:43:00.210
And people did discuss
the ways to treat it.

00:43:00.210 --> 00:43:03.180
In fact, one way,
for example, is

00:43:03.180 --> 00:43:09.210
to add a thermostat to the
molecular dynamics simulation.

00:43:09.210 --> 00:43:11.500
And there are different
ways to add a thermostat

00:43:11.500 --> 00:43:15.540
so you can actually add an
additional equation of motion

00:43:15.540 --> 00:43:20.025
to simulate the environment,
so that, as a whole,

00:43:20.025 --> 00:43:23.040
you can still have
self-consistency.

00:43:23.040 --> 00:43:25.530
So there are different
thermostat constants.

00:43:25.530 --> 00:43:27.540
The temperature
thermostat, they're

00:43:27.540 --> 00:43:30.420
are even constant
pressure thermostats--

00:43:30.420 --> 00:43:32.770
but if you go to
check all this paper,

00:43:32.770 --> 00:43:36.570
it actually invariably
comes to those basic

00:43:36.570 --> 00:43:39.570
how you can go from
one ensemble result

00:43:39.570 --> 00:43:43.710
to another ensemble result.

00:43:43.710 --> 00:43:47.730
So I spent a lot
of time in talking

00:43:47.730 --> 00:43:52.550
going from microcanonical
to canonical, right?

00:43:52.550 --> 00:43:57.260
The nest one here
is a closed system

00:43:57.260 --> 00:43:58.410
but a constant temperature.

00:43:58.410 --> 00:44:00.770
So energy exchange
with the environment.

00:44:00.770 --> 00:44:04.460
Now if you have an open system
and a constant temperature,

00:44:04.460 --> 00:44:06.590
there is both energy
and particle exchange

00:44:06.590 --> 00:44:08.730
with the environment.

00:44:08.730 --> 00:44:11.820
And you do the same thing.

00:44:11.820 --> 00:44:16.940
So you will find that
the last probability--

00:44:16.940 --> 00:44:19.520
I'm not going to derive it now.

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:38.560
The grand canonical, and the
probability of a quantum system

00:44:38.560 --> 00:44:41.050
has energy Ei.

00:44:41.050 --> 00:44:43.750
Has Ni particles.

00:44:43.750 --> 00:44:52.300
It's exponential minus
Ei, minus mu, Ni, kbT.

00:44:52.300 --> 00:44:55.190
That's why we used that before.

00:44:55.190 --> 00:44:57.910
And divide that by another
partition function.

00:44:57.910 --> 00:45:01.615
This one, this Z is called
a partition function.

00:45:10.200 --> 00:45:12.840
The same Z there is
partition function.

00:45:12.840 --> 00:45:18.390
And say the factor here,
this is what at the start

00:45:18.390 --> 00:45:19.750
I said was a Gibbs factor.

00:45:24.960 --> 00:45:27.140
That's how we derived
Fermi-Dirac and Einstein

00:45:27.140 --> 00:45:27.740
distribution.

00:45:27.740 --> 00:45:32.160
We start with this
probability, and then we

00:45:32.160 --> 00:45:37.810
say normalization that will
give me the partition function

00:45:37.810 --> 00:45:39.370
there.

00:45:39.370 --> 00:45:42.525
The normalization factor,
I used to use the symbol A.

00:45:42.525 --> 00:45:46.930
But here it's symbol Z.
And the same thing here.

00:45:46.930 --> 00:45:50.940
So this is a Boltzmann
factor for canonical.

00:45:50.940 --> 00:45:54.450
That's the Gibbs factor
for grand canonical,

00:45:54.450 --> 00:45:57.380
for an open system.

00:45:57.380 --> 00:46:01.100
So this is essentially--
what did I say?

00:46:01.100 --> 00:46:03.530
It's a statistical
foundation, and

00:46:03.530 --> 00:46:05.370
a beautiful
mathematical treatment,

00:46:05.370 --> 00:46:08.160
once you start with
microcanonical.

00:46:08.160 --> 00:46:11.220
And it's pretty--
it's a deep concept.

00:46:11.220 --> 00:46:15.070
So I'm not asking you to--

00:46:15.070 --> 00:46:16.760
I said I'm not
going to test you,

00:46:16.760 --> 00:46:18.880
but I want you to
know the terminology.

00:46:21.880 --> 00:46:27.655
Now let's look at a few
examples of how we use this.

00:46:47.610 --> 00:46:54.220
Let's apply this
to a few problems.

00:46:54.220 --> 00:46:56.820
And I discussed the electrons.

00:46:56.820 --> 00:47:00.070
I discussed [INAUDIBLE],,
Fermi-Dirac, Bose-Einstein.

00:47:00.070 --> 00:47:02.560
And now let's go
back to gas molecule.

00:47:16.090 --> 00:47:19.120
Right, if you
recall in chapter 1,

00:47:19.120 --> 00:47:21.640
we discussed the
Maxwell distribution.

00:47:21.640 --> 00:47:22.330
Velocity.

00:47:22.330 --> 00:47:31.780
We said, OK, the energy of
a molecule is one half m,

00:47:31.780 --> 00:47:37.810
Vx squared, plus Vy
squared, plus Vz squared.

00:47:37.810 --> 00:47:42.160
But you could also do quantum
particle in box solution.

00:47:42.160 --> 00:47:42.970
We saw this.

00:47:42.970 --> 00:47:45.310
When you have a
particle in your box,

00:47:45.310 --> 00:47:54.910
and that is h bar squared 2m,
Kx squared, plus Ky squared,

00:47:54.910 --> 00:47:57.070
plus Kz squared.

00:47:57.070 --> 00:48:01.630
You can do either
quantum or classical.

00:48:01.630 --> 00:48:05.770
I'm going to actually use
the quantum notation here.

00:48:05.770 --> 00:48:10.510
This is essentially-- the
h, k, gives you momentum.

00:48:10.510 --> 00:48:12.010
P squared to m.

00:48:12.010 --> 00:48:14.620
That's the quantum here.

00:48:14.620 --> 00:48:19.060
And of course,
given the energy, I

00:48:19.060 --> 00:48:23.410
can go to look at
this as a system

00:48:23.410 --> 00:48:25.040
at a constant temperature.

00:48:25.040 --> 00:48:30.430
So I can use the canonical
example and the partition

00:48:30.430 --> 00:48:32.800
function for one particle.

00:48:42.180 --> 00:48:47.400
For one particle it's Z1,
and that will be summing up

00:48:47.400 --> 00:48:51.240
all possible Kx, Ky, Kz.

00:48:51.240 --> 00:48:54.800
Kx, Ky, Kz.

00:48:54.800 --> 00:48:57.660
And so it sums up
all energy levels

00:48:57.660 --> 00:49:05.600
as what we were showing in the--

00:49:05.600 --> 00:49:09.010
where did it [INAUDIBLE].

00:49:09.010 --> 00:49:14.220
Right, Z is the sum of
all energy quantum states.

00:49:14.220 --> 00:49:17.370
So sum of all quantum states.

00:49:17.370 --> 00:49:25.950
And the exponential Ei, which is
a function of Kx, Ky, Kz, kbT.

00:49:30.200 --> 00:49:42.750
So this is my
partition function Z.

00:49:42.750 --> 00:49:45.660
And if I want to know
what's the average energy--

00:49:55.710 --> 00:50:01.740
I have my average energy, E
is just, again, a summation.

00:50:01.740 --> 00:50:06.900
I can write a Kx, Ky, Kz, rather
than three summation symbols.

00:50:06.900 --> 00:50:11.220
And this is the E as
this combination of--

00:50:11.220 --> 00:50:16.800
for each state, it's how much
energy and the probability

00:50:16.800 --> 00:50:23.220
for this quantum state.

00:50:23.220 --> 00:50:30.490
That probability is what I
have here, Ei, kbT over Z.

00:50:30.490 --> 00:50:34.760
So I have the
probability expression,

00:50:34.760 --> 00:50:39.470
I have the energy expression,
I can go to find that average.

00:50:42.090 --> 00:50:43.950
But there is a
simpler way, rather

00:50:43.950 --> 00:50:45.960
than doing this summation.

00:50:45.960 --> 00:50:49.470
And you can actually
prove easily,

00:50:49.470 --> 00:50:53.460
if I take a
derivative of this Z,

00:50:53.460 --> 00:50:57.360
you say this one is
actually equals d

00:50:57.360 --> 00:51:03.090
log Z1, dy, where y equals kbT.

00:51:03.090 --> 00:51:05.310
That's just the
mathematical result.

00:51:05.310 --> 00:51:08.550
And for convenience,
if I find this

00:51:08.550 --> 00:51:12.320
I can just take the
derivative of Z1.

00:51:17.950 --> 00:51:24.460
If you see, because if I do
a derivative, I will log--

00:51:24.460 --> 00:51:29.200
I say this is in the
denominator, that gives me Z.

00:51:29.200 --> 00:51:33.620
And the numerator essentially
gives me probability.

00:51:33.620 --> 00:51:37.020
And if I take kbT as--

00:51:37.020 --> 00:51:40.940
Oh, 1 over-- this should be.

00:51:40.940 --> 00:51:43.860
That y should be--

00:51:43.860 --> 00:51:48.000
No, I think actually
dy should give me--

00:51:48.000 --> 00:51:53.020
y should be 1 over
kbT, rather than kbT.

00:51:53.020 --> 00:51:56.050
So if you do that, you'll
get the E in the front.

00:51:56.050 --> 00:51:57.280
That's the E in the front.

00:52:01.100 --> 00:52:06.680
So that means I do not need
to actually do this summation.

00:52:06.680 --> 00:52:10.990
If I just find the Z1,
my problem is solved.

00:52:10.990 --> 00:52:14.980
And then to do Z1, I
can word the summation.

00:52:14.980 --> 00:52:19.390
If this one doesn't
change rapidly,

00:52:19.390 --> 00:52:21.610
I do my summation
into integration

00:52:21.610 --> 00:52:23.740
using density of states.

00:52:23.740 --> 00:52:27.340
If it changes rapidly,
I cannot do that.

00:52:27.340 --> 00:52:32.920
So if it's a very-- if the
states are far apart, or a kT,

00:52:32.920 --> 00:52:36.770
I cannot do that
integration to summation.

00:52:36.770 --> 00:52:39.050
Its summation to integration.

00:52:39.050 --> 00:52:45.100
So now, because these states
are closely spaced, I can do it.

00:52:51.180 --> 00:52:55.710
So you find out Z1.

00:52:55.710 --> 00:53:00.420
From this I can actually
do my integration.

00:53:00.420 --> 00:53:06.750
This exponential E, kbT
over density of states,

00:53:06.750 --> 00:53:11.640
dE, as 0 to infinite,
and this density

00:53:11.640 --> 00:53:14.610
of states for
particles in the box,

00:53:14.610 --> 00:53:18.510
given this dispersion
energy with vector relation,

00:53:18.510 --> 00:53:22.700
you'll find that
there is a way for--

00:53:22.700 --> 00:53:26.460
we did this before, so I'm
not going to re-derive it.

00:53:26.460 --> 00:53:32.190
h squared, 2m, h
squared and 3/2.

00:53:32.190 --> 00:53:34.650
We did it in the
context of electrons.

00:53:34.650 --> 00:53:36.960
But they could be the
same for molecules.

00:53:41.650 --> 00:53:46.630
So you substitute the density
upstage energy into it,

00:53:46.630 --> 00:53:53.290
and you find out this
one equals V, the volume,

00:53:53.290 --> 00:53:59.395
divided by lambda cubic,
where this lambda cubic is--

00:54:02.160 --> 00:54:03.880
Or lambda itself.

00:54:03.880 --> 00:54:04.840
Not lambda cubic.

00:54:04.840 --> 00:54:05.530
Lambda itself.

00:54:10.710 --> 00:54:14.595
h squared, 2pi, mkbT.

00:54:17.640 --> 00:54:21.090
So this is your lambda.

00:54:21.090 --> 00:54:26.310
And this lambda is called the
thermal de Broglie wavelengths.

00:54:26.310 --> 00:54:28.550
It's the thermal de
Broglie wavelengths.

00:54:34.340 --> 00:54:39.960
De Broglie wavelengths.

00:54:45.810 --> 00:54:48.510
Why is it called thermal
de Broglie wavelengths?

00:54:48.510 --> 00:54:54.240
Basically if you think
about your energy as kbT,

00:54:54.240 --> 00:54:58.710
you go from that energy
to momentum because kbT,

00:54:58.710 --> 00:55:06.240
1/2 kbT energy equals momentum
P squared divide by 2m.

00:55:06.240 --> 00:55:09.960
From momentum you go
convert it into wavelengths.

00:55:09.960 --> 00:55:13.580
That's what you will get.

00:55:13.580 --> 00:55:16.840
So it's called thermal
de Broglie wavelengths.

00:55:16.840 --> 00:55:22.750
So from Z, if I go into here
because y is 1 over kbT,

00:55:22.750 --> 00:55:25.510
so you take a log, and
you take a derivative,

00:55:25.510 --> 00:55:34.990
you find the average energy of
this equals mass d log Z, dy,

00:55:34.990 --> 00:55:38.530
and equals 3/2 kbT.

00:55:38.530 --> 00:55:43.960
That's the result
we found when we

00:55:43.960 --> 00:55:47.680
did the Maxwell distribution.

00:55:47.680 --> 00:55:51.640
We came back to the same result,
but through a different--

00:55:51.640 --> 00:55:56.440
this is a more rigorous
statistical approach foundation

00:55:56.440 --> 00:55:57.690
there.

00:55:57.690 --> 00:56:05.020
And it says the average energy
of a particle is 3/2 kbT.

00:56:05.020 --> 00:56:11.620
And recall, we commented
the equal partition, if you

00:56:11.620 --> 00:56:16.450
have quadratic determined
energy, here is 1/2 m,

00:56:16.450 --> 00:56:17.450
Vx squared.

00:56:17.450 --> 00:56:18.820
Or kx squared.

00:56:18.820 --> 00:56:20.770
That's quadratic term.

00:56:20.770 --> 00:56:22.530
Right?

00:56:22.530 --> 00:56:26.340
And if this energy does
not separate that far part,

00:56:26.340 --> 00:56:33.590
at a high temperature, each
mode contributed 1/2 kbT.

00:56:33.590 --> 00:56:34.970
That's equal partition.

00:56:34.970 --> 00:56:38.950
So I have three modes
here, I have 3/2 kbT.

00:56:45.440 --> 00:56:50.390
Of course, from here the
specific heat of one molecule

00:56:50.390 --> 00:56:52.850
is du, de, dt.

00:56:52.850 --> 00:56:55.380
So it's 3/2 kb.

00:56:55.380 --> 00:56:56.010
Right?

00:56:56.010 --> 00:56:59.940
Specifically the one
molecule is 3/2 kb.

00:57:03.020 --> 00:57:04.360
And if it's one more--

00:57:07.480 --> 00:57:13.570
one more is 3/2 kb times
Avogadro constraint.

00:57:13.570 --> 00:57:18.080
This too gives you the
universal gas constant.

00:57:18.080 --> 00:57:26.570
That's what you know from
thermodynamics, is that 3/2 R.

00:57:26.570 --> 00:57:29.450
But you say, no,
that's not all 3/2,

00:57:29.450 --> 00:57:32.340
that's only a monatomic gas.

00:57:32.340 --> 00:57:34.010
What about diatomic gas?

00:57:37.270 --> 00:57:38.290
Who will remember that.

00:57:38.290 --> 00:57:40.410
What's the specific
heat of diatomic gas?

00:57:43.735 --> 00:57:47.070
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:57:47.070 --> 00:57:49.080
GANG CHEN: 5, right?

00:57:49.080 --> 00:57:55.290
5 hops R. So why is that?

00:57:55.290 --> 00:57:56.970
OK, so let's go to--

00:58:17.980 --> 00:58:19.690
This is only one molecule.

00:58:19.690 --> 00:58:24.010
Let me add one more comment.

00:58:24.010 --> 00:58:30.220
When I have dilute particles,
here I have-- before

00:58:30.220 --> 00:58:31.360
that was one molecule.

00:58:31.360 --> 00:58:47.235
If I have N molecules
and if my energy is, say,

00:58:47.235 --> 00:58:54.610
the summation of all the
molecule Ei, and the kbT.

00:59:01.050 --> 00:59:07.020
And the question is how
to do this summation.

00:59:07.020 --> 00:59:12.790
It it's dilute
molecules, that means--

00:59:12.790 --> 00:59:17.710
diluted means really the energy
levels of those molecules

00:59:17.710 --> 00:59:19.790
do not affect each other.

00:59:19.790 --> 00:59:23.950
So my individual molecule
solution is pretty much valid,

00:59:23.950 --> 00:59:28.650
and all the molecules have
the same energy levels.

00:59:28.650 --> 00:59:31.170
So in that case,
I will have-- this

00:59:31.170 --> 00:59:34.020
is for each identical
quantum state.

00:59:34.020 --> 00:59:35.010
Ei.

00:59:35.010 --> 00:59:36.580
I have N molecules.

00:59:36.580 --> 00:59:40.590
So I have exponential
Z1 to the n-th power.

00:59:46.290 --> 00:59:52.860
But there is one trick
that says the molecules are

00:59:52.860 --> 00:59:53.880
indistinguishable.

00:59:53.880 --> 00:59:58.840
If you do a combination, if
it's indistinguishable objects,

00:59:58.840 --> 01:00:00.790
What do you do?

01:00:00.790 --> 01:00:03.670
The probability
of counting them?

01:00:06.212 --> 01:00:07.670
AUDIENCE: You write
that and then--

01:00:07.670 --> 01:00:10.580
GANG CHEN: So you'll
divide it by N factorial.

01:00:15.640 --> 01:00:20.680
So that's if it's
indistinguishable.

01:00:20.680 --> 01:00:25.780
But when they are, it's dilute.

01:00:25.780 --> 01:00:27.450
Right?

01:00:27.450 --> 01:00:31.095
So when I say dilute, I say
the molecule to not affect

01:00:31.095 --> 01:00:32.220
each other's energy levels.

01:00:41.870 --> 01:00:46.760
When I say dilute, one
condition I can use

01:00:46.760 --> 01:00:52.090
is the number of
states of one molecule

01:00:52.090 --> 01:00:57.760
in the box is much larger
than the number of particles.

01:00:57.760 --> 01:01:01.570
The Lambert quantum
state of one molecule

01:01:01.570 --> 01:01:07.560
is much larger than the
number of particles.

01:01:07.560 --> 01:01:10.270
So the particles do not
influence each other.

01:01:10.270 --> 01:01:12.450
And so the number
of states, it's

01:01:12.450 --> 01:01:16.500
really Kx, Ky, Kz, times 1.

01:01:16.500 --> 01:01:21.160
Each combination of Kx,
Ky, Kz gives me one state.

01:01:21.160 --> 01:01:29.050
So I have the density of states
integrated over all energy.

01:01:29.050 --> 01:01:32.890
And if the energy is 0,
I can say roughly it's

01:01:32.890 --> 01:01:38.550
3, because the average
energy is 3/2 kbT.

01:01:38.550 --> 01:01:43.050
And that gives me much
larger than the total number

01:01:43.050 --> 01:01:44.590
of molecules.

01:01:44.590 --> 01:01:47.430
And this actually
gives me the condition,

01:01:47.430 --> 01:01:51.600
if you do your math again,
we have [INAUDIBLE] states

01:01:51.600 --> 01:02:01.230
and weigh pi over 6, and 1/2
lambda cubic, much less than 1.

01:02:01.230 --> 01:02:03.540
So that's the mathematics.

01:02:03.540 --> 01:02:07.150
What it means, you say lambda
cubic, lambda is the de Broglie

01:02:07.150 --> 01:02:09.240
wavelengths, right?

01:02:09.240 --> 01:02:14.490
So V divide by N, if I move V
to the end of this side, that's

01:02:14.490 --> 01:02:17.890
the volume of one particle.

01:02:17.890 --> 01:02:22.200
It's much larger than the
thermal de Broglie wavelengths.

01:02:22.200 --> 01:02:26.100
So the wave of
each atom does not

01:02:26.100 --> 01:02:30.550
overlap with the other atoms.

01:02:30.550 --> 01:02:34.120
So that's the particle dilution.

01:02:34.120 --> 01:02:37.700
Dilute particles.

01:02:37.700 --> 01:02:44.180
OK, so how I go from
here to 3/5 kbT.

01:02:44.180 --> 01:02:47.960
And if I have the
total energy here,

01:02:47.960 --> 01:02:50.790
we only consider
the translation.

01:02:50.790 --> 01:02:55.430
So if I have the energy
of one particular, Ei,

01:02:55.430 --> 01:02:57.920
here we have a translation.

01:02:57.920 --> 01:03:01.640
Plus you can also have
rotation of the particle.

01:03:01.640 --> 01:03:04.850
You can have the
vibration of the particle.

01:03:09.870 --> 01:03:10.920
And the electronic.

01:03:16.790 --> 01:03:23.900
So the total partition function
for N molecules is really

01:03:23.900 --> 01:03:25.370
Z of the--

01:03:25.370 --> 01:03:35.050
here we have translation,
and rotation, and vibration,

01:03:35.050 --> 01:03:38.050
and the electronic
partition function.

01:03:38.050 --> 01:03:43.117
Each of those to the n-th power
and divided by N factorial.

01:03:47.650 --> 01:03:53.950
And from this, En, you go
to take the average energy--

01:03:53.950 --> 01:03:55.953
once you find that Z--

01:03:55.953 --> 01:03:58.120
you take the average energy
and take the temperature

01:03:58.120 --> 01:04:00.660
derivative.

01:04:00.660 --> 01:04:03.960
So it really depends
on, at the end--

01:04:03.960 --> 01:04:07.230
because it's a
Boltzmann factor, Ei,

01:04:07.230 --> 01:04:12.542
my electronic energy is
a few electron volts.

01:04:12.542 --> 01:04:13.500
Let's think about this.

01:04:13.500 --> 01:04:16.020
What's this [INAUDIBLE] here?

01:04:16.020 --> 01:04:19.690
Electrons, the energy level--
this is a simple hydrogen

01:04:19.690 --> 01:04:20.190
model.

01:04:20.190 --> 01:04:24.660
This is minus 13.6 [INAUDIBLE].

01:04:24.660 --> 01:04:31.240
This is a minus 13.6 divide
by 4 because it's N squared.

01:04:31.240 --> 01:04:32.620
That's a big gap here.

01:04:35.170 --> 01:04:38.800
So the electronic
energy normally

01:04:38.800 --> 01:04:45.820
is not excited, because the
Ei over kbT is a huge number.

01:04:45.820 --> 01:04:47.670
And this [INAUDIBLE].

01:04:47.670 --> 01:04:51.720
So electrons do not go
to a higher energy level,

01:04:51.720 --> 01:04:54.420
and they do not contribute
to specific heat

01:04:54.420 --> 01:04:55.620
at room temperature.

01:04:55.620 --> 01:04:58.073
Because when temperatures
change, their energy--

01:04:58.073 --> 01:04:59.490
they're still at
the ground state.

01:05:03.710 --> 01:05:10.190
And vibration energy
level, what do you

01:05:10.190 --> 01:05:12.470
know about the vibration
frequency of hydrogen?

01:05:16.330 --> 01:05:19.150
If you think about the
gas atmospheric absorption

01:05:19.150 --> 01:05:21.820
spectrum, right?

01:05:21.820 --> 01:05:26.470
And in the infrared,
a few microns,

01:05:26.470 --> 01:05:29.380
you got a lot of lines, right?

01:05:29.380 --> 01:05:33.370
Those lines are the
vibrational energy levels.

01:05:33.370 --> 01:05:35.240
Vibration plus rotation.

01:05:35.240 --> 01:05:36.880
Rotation is a small energy.

01:05:36.880 --> 01:05:41.050
Vibration is actually-- this
is many rotations attached

01:05:41.050 --> 01:05:44.120
to vibration.

01:05:44.120 --> 01:05:48.320
So if vibration is a few
microns, that means about 0.2,

01:05:48.320 --> 01:05:53.390
0.3, to 0.5 electron volts.

01:05:53.390 --> 01:05:54.560
Few microns.

01:05:54.560 --> 01:05:58.940
1 micron is 1.24 electron volts.

01:05:58.940 --> 01:06:03.960
Right so if it's 2
micron, it's 0.6.

01:06:03.960 --> 01:06:07.200
4 micron, 0.3 electron volts.

01:06:10.480 --> 01:06:15.960
kT is 26 million electron volts.

01:06:15.960 --> 01:06:19.040
26 million electron volts.

01:06:19.040 --> 01:06:20.680
So when you have--

01:06:20.680 --> 01:06:26.370
this is a 0.3 divided by 0.03,
that exponential is minus 10.

01:06:26.370 --> 01:06:30.035
So normally vibration at room
temperature is not excited.

01:06:33.180 --> 01:06:38.600
And rotation turns out
very low temperature.

01:06:38.600 --> 01:06:40.940
Just the rotation itself.

01:06:40.940 --> 01:06:44.650
The energy needed to
rotate the object.

01:06:44.650 --> 01:06:48.490
And that's the
equivalent temperature--

01:06:48.490 --> 01:06:51.400
It's about 80 Kelvin.

01:06:51.400 --> 01:06:53.650
So at room temperature,
the rotation,

01:06:53.650 --> 01:06:57.370
the molecule rotational
modes, all rotation modes,

01:06:57.370 --> 01:06:59.370
are excited.

01:06:59.370 --> 01:07:03.890
And if you think about rotation,
there's two degrees of freedom.

01:07:03.890 --> 01:07:04.390
Right?

01:07:04.390 --> 01:07:07.140
Theta phi.

01:07:07.140 --> 01:07:10.590
The energy is 1/2
omega, I squared.

01:07:10.590 --> 01:07:11.190
No.

01:07:11.190 --> 01:07:13.790
1/2 I omega squared.

01:07:13.790 --> 01:07:15.800
It's a quadratic.

01:07:15.800 --> 01:07:17.600
The equity partition
says there will

01:07:17.600 --> 01:07:22.810
be another lot 2, times 1/2 kT.

01:07:22.810 --> 01:07:29.080
So if you go to look at
the specific heat of a gas,

01:07:29.080 --> 01:07:34.480
it's actually not
just a stat function.

01:07:34.480 --> 01:07:37.630
It really depends on
what the temperature

01:07:37.630 --> 01:07:40.900
range you're dealing with is.

01:07:40.900 --> 01:07:46.840
And specific heat of constant
volume-- specific heat.

01:07:46.840 --> 01:07:48.340
This is the temperature.

01:07:48.340 --> 01:07:55.640
And the translation is easily
excited motion translation.

01:07:55.640 --> 01:08:00.235
So this is 3/2 R, universal
gas constant per Moore.

01:08:04.430 --> 01:08:09.280
And around 80 Kelvin, the
rotations start to kick in.

01:08:09.280 --> 01:08:10.800
So there is a transition range.

01:08:15.380 --> 01:08:18.880
And when you go to
higher temperature,

01:08:18.880 --> 01:08:22.870
all the rotational
energy is fully excited.

01:08:22.870 --> 01:08:25.310
Equity partition
begins to be valid.

01:08:25.310 --> 01:08:28.090
It's another sharp
transition there.

01:08:28.090 --> 01:08:32.880
So here you got 5/2 Ru.

01:08:32.880 --> 01:08:39.660
That's where you say when
you do your gas calculation,

01:08:39.660 --> 01:08:41.579
you say this
specifically is 5/2.

01:08:45.160 --> 01:08:49.670
And when you go to
thousands of degrees,

01:08:49.670 --> 01:08:52.640
you add another vibration.

01:08:52.640 --> 01:08:57.529
It's 1/2 Nv squared,
plus 1/2 Kx squared.

01:08:57.529 --> 01:09:00.080
So two quadratic terms.

01:09:00.080 --> 01:09:03.810
You add another 2 times 1/2 kbT.

01:09:03.810 --> 01:09:05.279
Equal partition.

01:09:05.279 --> 01:09:09.850
But this will require you to
go to the thousandth degree.

01:09:09.850 --> 01:09:15.630
So for gas you can go to--

01:09:15.630 --> 01:09:17.010
it's a diatomic gas.

01:09:17.010 --> 01:09:19.529
Go to 7/2.

01:09:19.529 --> 01:09:22.109
If you have three
atoms, you have

01:09:22.109 --> 01:09:27.205
to look at more of those
different modes per molecule.

01:09:30.380 --> 01:09:36.710
So that's the discussion
on specific heat.

01:09:36.710 --> 01:09:41.370
And I think that
what's really for you

01:09:41.370 --> 01:09:43.380
probably the most
useful is understanding

01:09:43.380 --> 01:09:48.240
